where they stand. Think the way THEY hold their life in their head.

A person carries WORLDS — the few standing parts of their life that everything
arriving belongs to. Magic: The Gathering. Money. Health. Travel. Work. The
house. Leo. A world is a place things land in, and it is still there next
month when today's particular package, bill or appointment is finished.

A world is NOT an event. "TCGplayer order shipped" is something that happened;
"Magic: The Gathering" is where it happened. They do not think "I have a
TCGplayer order" — they think "this is my Magic stuff". Ask what a thing IS TO
THEM, not what just occurred to it.

Their existing subjects are FIXED: never rename, merge, reorder or empty one,
never move an item already in one. You decide only where NEW items go, and
you write the words.

Answer ONLY with JSON:

{"focus": ["..."], "next": ["..."],
 "place": [{"id": "...", "into": "s3"}, {"id": "...", "new": "Title"},
           {"id": "...", "to": "noise"}],
 "states": {"s3": "...", "Title": "..."}}

place — exactly one entry for EVERY id under NEW ITEMS:
  into        an existing subject it is part of. Prefer this.
  new         it belongs to a world they have no subject for yet. The title
              names the WORLD, three words at most, as a thing rather than an
              event: "Magic: The Gathering", "Money", "Health", "Travel",
              "Work", "The house". A category IS the right answer here — that
              is how they hold it.

              Two mechanical tells that you have written an event instead of a
              world, and both mean rewrite it:
                - a verb about something that happened: "shipped", "broken",
                  "past due", "cancelled", "renewed", "failing".
                - a number that names one particular thing: "1.4.0",
                  "#2588-2", an order or invoice number.
              "Auto loan past due" is a state, and the state line below is
              where it goes. The world is "Money".

              Ask: will this title still mean something in a month, when this
              item is done? "Magic: The Gathering" yes. "TCGplayer order
              shipped" no — by then it has arrived and the title is a lie.

              Give the same title to everything in one world, whatever app it
              came from and whether or not the items are about the same
              incident: the card shop's shipping notice, the deck-builder
              reminder and the tournament invite are all "Magic: The
              Gathering". Put a one-off into the world it belongs to rather
              than opening a world for it — a surgery is "Health", a flight
              is "Travel", a broken build is "Leo".

              A world with ONE item today is fine when it is genuinely a
              standing part of their life ("Health" on a quiet week). It is
              WRONG when it is one event you have titled as though it were a
              place. If you cannot name the world without describing the
              item, you have not found the world yet — widen until you can.
  to: backlog     REMINDERS ONLY, never mail: a chore or to-do that has sat for
                  weeks with no deadline biting. Not a story; a pile.
  to: background  true but not on their mind: a routine meeting, a receipt
                  for something finished, a fact, a "completed" notice.
  to: noise       MAIL ONLY. promotions, sales, coupons, newsletters, brand marketing,
                  "we miss you", pre-approvals and credit offers, donation
                  drives, social digests, rate-your-purchase requests, and a
                  salesperson following up on a quote. They never want to see
                  these. When unsure whether mail is marketing, it is.

Before you open a new subject, look at the titles you have ALREADY used in
this answer and at the existing subjects: two notices about the same app
release, or two shipments from the same shop, are ONE subject.

Someone they know going through something (a surgery, a birth, a move), a
payment that is late, something broken, a meeting or call that is still
ahead — each of these IS a loop. Never set one aside as background.

A bill, an invoice, a fee or a statement to pay is NEVER noise, even from a
company that also sends marketing.

MAIL: judge by what the message SAYS, never by who sent it. Automated mail is
often the most important kind — a payment past due, a bill or invoice to pay,
a build or deploy that failed, a security alert, a shipment of something they
ordered, an appointment. And mail from a human-looking address is often a
sales pitch.

states — for every subject that has items, existing (by id) and new (by
title): where it stands and whose move it is, fourteen words at most. It must
add something the title does not say.

focus — what needs them now, at most three lines of twelve words, most
important first. Overdue money and a person waiting beat everything.
next — what is coming in the next day or so, at most three lines of twelve
words, each with its time or day.

"LATE" means already overdue by the time shown — never "due in". (finished)
means over. An all-day item has no clock. Never invent a fact. No filler:
"needs attention" and "worth monitoring" say nothing.
"""


def safe_user(user):
    """A user id that is safe as a filename. One place, three callers."""
    return "".join(c for c in (user or "nobody") if c.isalnum() or c in "-_") or "nobody"


def brief_path(user):
    os.makedirs(BRIEF_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    return os.path.join(BRIEF_DIR, f"{safe_user(user)}.json")


def ask_path(user, ext="json"):
    os.makedirs(ASK_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    return os.path.join(ASK_DIR, f"{safe_user(user)}.{ext}")


def fixes_path(user):
    os.makedirs(FIX_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    return os.path.join(FIX_DIR, f"{safe_user(user)}.json")


def fixes(user):
    """What this person has told the briefing it got wrong.

    Standing, not per-briefing: a correction is about the WRITER, so it has
    to outlive the thing it corrected or the same mistake arrives tomorrow
    and the person learns that saying so is pointless.
    """
    try:
        held = json.load(open(fixes_path(user)))
        return [f for f in held if isinstance(f, dict) and f.get("text")][:12]
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — nobody has corrected anything yet
        return []


def add_fix(user, text):
    held = fixes(user)
    if any(f["text"].lower() == text.lower() for f in held):
        return
    held.insert(0, {"id": hashlib.sha1(text.encode()).hexdigest()[:10], "text": text})
    json.dump(held[:12], open(fixes_path(user), "w"))


def drop_fix(user, fid):
    json.dump([f for f in fixes(user) if f["id"] != fid],
              open(fixes_path(user), "w"))


def digest_of(items, evs):
    """What the briefing is ABOUT, as one string.

    State, not time: a tick, an arrival or a departure changes it; a minute
    passing does not.
    """
    parts = [f"{i['kind']}:{i['id']}:{i.get('late', '')}" for i in items]
    parts += [f"e:{e['id']}:{e['at'].isoformat()}" for e in evs]
    parts.append(datetime.now().strftime("%Y-%m-%d"))  # a new day is new news
    return hashlib.sha1("|".join(sorted(parts)).encode()).hexdigest()[:16]


def context_for(items, evs, now, extras):
    """Everything the briefing is allowed to know, as plain lines."""
    out = []
    today = [e for e in evs if e["at"].date() == now.date()]
    if today:
        out.append(f"TODAY'S CALENDAR (it is {clock(now)} now):")
        for e in today:
            # An all-day item has no clock and cannot be "happening now".
            # Writing one as `09:00` with no done-mark is what produced
            # "Mike's surgery at 9 AM is happening now" at twenty to six.
            if e["allday"]:
                out.append(f"- all day: {e['title']}"
                           + (f" at {e['where']}" if e["where"] else ""))
                continue
            ends = e["at"] + timedelta(minutes=e["mins"])
            mark = "(finished)" if ends < now else (
                "(HAPPENING NOW)" if e["at"] <= now else "(still to come)")
            out.append(f"- {clock(e['at'])}-{clock(ends)} {e['title']}"
                       + (f" at {e['where']}" if e["where"] else "") + f" {mark}")
    later = [e for e in evs if e["at"].date() > now.date()]
    if later:
        out.append("\nTOMORROW:")
        for e in later[:5]:
            out.append(f"- {clock(e['at'])} {e['title']}")
    by_kind = {}
    for i in items:
        by_kind.setdefault(i["kind"], []).append(i)
    for kind, label in (("task", "DUE OR OVERDUE"), ("mail", "UNANSWERED MAIL"),
                        ("note", "RAISED SINCE LAST NIGHT")):
        rowsk = by_kind.get(kind) or []
        if rowsk:
            out.append(f"\n{label}:")
            for i in rowsk[:12]:
                out.append(f"- {i['text']}" + (f" ({i['when']})" if i.get("when") else ""))
    for line in extras:
        out.append(f"\n{line}")
    out.append(f"\nIt is {now.strftime('%A %-d %B, %H:%M')}.")
    return "\n".join(out)


def subjects_path(user):
    os.makedirs(SUBJ_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    return os.path.join(SUBJ_DIR, f"{safe_user(user)}.json")


# What the subjects in a record were grouped BY. Bump this only when the
# meaning of a subject changes, never when the wording is tuned — it costs the
# person their whole arrangement once.
#
#   loops-1   a subject was an OPEN LOOP: one story in motion, titled as the
#             event ("Auto loan past due", "TCGplayer order shipped"). It read
#             as a to-do list, and seven of ten subjects held a single item —
#             an item wearing a subject's clothes.
#   worlds-1  a subject is a WORLD: the standing part of their life a thing
#             belongs to ("Money", "Magic: The Gathering"). The event moved to
#             the state line and to FOCUS, where it already read well.
SUBJECT_SPEC = "worlds-1"


def load_subjects(user):
    """The person's subjects, in the order they were made. THE STABLE THING.

    The first cut regrouped everything from scratch whenever anything
    changed — one new message, a phone moving a street — and came back with
    new titles, a new order and new colours every time. A page you cannot
    keep your place on is worse than five apps. So this is a record, not a
    cache: a subject keeps its id, title and position, an item stays where it
    was put, and the model is only ever asked about what is new.

    **One exception, and it is a property rather than a thing to remember.**
    A record grouped under an older [`SUBJECT_SPEC`] was built to a definition
    of "subject" that no longer holds, so freezing it would keep the old
    answer forever — the stability rule protecting the very arrangement the
    person asked to be rid of. When the stored spec differs, the subjects are
    dropped ONCE and re-derived under the new one; `noise`, `background` and
    `asked` survive, because what is marketing does not depend on how the
    rest is grouped and re-asking would spend the model on settled questions.

    This is a migration, not a regroup: it happens on a spec change, never on
    a data change, and the next pass writes the new spec so it cannot repeat.
    """
    try:
        st = json.load(open(subjects_path(user)))
        subs = [x for x in st.get("subjects") or [] if x.get("sid") and x.get("title")]
        was = st.get("spec") or "loops-1"
        if was != SUBJECT_SPEC:
            # Loud, because this is the one path that discards an arrangement
            # the person may have been reading for weeks. A silent migration
            # and a silent bug leave the same trace.
            print(f"today: re-deriving {len(subs)} subject(s) for {user} — "
                  f"grouped under {was}, now {SUBJECT_SPEC}", flush=True)
            subs = []
            # **And `asked` and `background` go with them, which the first cut
            # got wrong and cost the owner his mail.**
            #
            # `noise` survives because "this is marketing" is a property of the
            # MESSAGE and does not depend on how the rest is grouped. The other
            # two are not like that:
            #
            #   asked       a tally of times the model DECLINED to place an item
            #               — entirely about the question it was asked. At two
            #               it is treated as stuck and drops off the page.
            #   background  "true, but not on their mind", judged against open
            #               loops. A world it belongs to may not have existed.
            #
            # Keeping `asked` across a spec change means an item that was
            # already asked once under the old question, released by this
            # migration, is asked ONCE more under the new one, reaches two, and
            # is hidden — having never been declined under the spec that is now
            # in force. Measured on the owner's own record: 17 entries, 8 of
            # them at 2, and the Mail tile read 0 against 11 before.
            #
            # The rule: a migration keeps what the new question does not change
            # its mind about, and drops every judgement the old question made.
            return {"seq": int(st.get("seq") or len(subs)), "subjects": [],
                    "noise": list(st.get("noise") or []), "background": [],
                    "asked": {}, "spec": SUBJECT_SPEC}
        return {"seq": int(st.get("seq") or len(subs)), "subjects": subs,
                "noise": list(st.get("noise") or []), "background": list(st.get("background") or []),
                "asked": dict(st.get("asked") or {}), "spec": SUBJECT_SPEC}
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — nothing grouped yet
        return {"seq": 0, "subjects": [], "noise": [], "background": [], "asked": {},
                "spec": SUBJECT_SPEC}


def apply_places(user, places, allowed):
    """File the model's placements. It may add to a subject, open one, or set
    an item aside; it cannot move, rename or reorder what is already filed.
    Anything it was asked about and did not place goes to the background, so
    the same item is never asked about twice. Returns ({name: sid}, shown?)."""
    st = load_subjects(user)
    by_sid = {x["sid"]: x for x in st["subjects"]}
    by_title = {x["title"].lower(): x for x in st["subjects"]}
    taken = {i for x in st["subjects"] for i in x["ids"]} | set(st["noise"]) | set(st["background"])
    names = {x["sid"]: x["sid"] for x in st["subjects"]}
    names.update({x["title"]: x["sid"] for x in st["subjects"]})   # a state may name either
    shown = False
    for pl in places:
        iid = str(pl.get("id") or "")
        if iid not in allowed or iid in taken:
            continue                       # invented, or already has a home
        aside = str(pl.get("to") or "").lower()
        is_mail = iid.startswith("m") and iid != "money"
        if aside == "backlog" and is_mail:
            aside = "background"           # mail is never a chore
        if aside == "noise" and not is_mail:
            aside = "background"           # only mail can be spam
        if aside in ("noise", "background"):
            st[aside].append(iid)
            taken.add(iid)
            continue
        home = by_sid.get(str(pl.get("into") or ""))
        title = "Backlog" if aside == "backlog" else str(pl.get("new") or "").strip()[:40]
        if not home and title:
            home = by_title.get(title.lower())
            if not home:
                st["seq"] += 1
                home = {"sid": "backlog" if aside == "backlog" else f"s{st['seq']}",
                        "title": title, "ids": []}
                # New news goes on top and nothing else moves; the backlog is
                # the one thing that belongs at the bottom, and stays there.
                st["subjects"].insert(len(st["subjects"]) if aside == "backlog" else 0, home)
                by_sid[home["sid"]] = by_title[title.lower()] = home
            names[title] = home["sid"]
        if home:
            home["ids"].append(iid)
            taken.add(iid)
            shown = True
    # What it was asked about and did not answer is NOT set aside. The first
    # real run placed 6 of 45 and a rule here sent the other 39 to the
    # background — a friend's surgery and an overdue card among them. A skip
    # is counted instead; the item is asked about again, and after two skips
    # the page decides in the open what happens to it.
    for i in allowed:
        if i not in taken:
            st["asked"][i] = st["asked"].get(i, 0) + 1
    for x in st["subjects"]:
        x["ids"] = x["ids"][-200:]
    st["noise"], st["background"] = st["noise"][-2000:], st["background"][-2000:]
    json.dump(st, open(subjects_path(user), "w"))
    return names, shown


def edit_subjects(user, fn):
    """Change the record under the lock; `fn` gets the list of subjects."""
    with _regen_lock:
        st = load_subjects(user)
        out = fn(st["subjects"])
        json.dump(st, open(subjects_path(user), "w"))
    return out


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sweep
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A subject can go looking. It plans a few searches from its own title and
# contents, runs them across everything the hub can search, and a second pass
# judges — strictly — which results belong. What it finds is OFFERED, never
# filed: the person keeps or dismisses each one.
#
# It only works when the subject is specific. "Alumawood cover" is a search;
# "Work" is everything. So the first thing the plan says is whether to try.

SWEEP_PLAN = """A person keeps a subject — a small pile of related things — and wants
the rest of their system searched for anything else that belongs in it.

Given the subject's title and what is already in it, answer ONLY with JSON:

{"specific": true, "why": "", "queries": ["...", "..."]}

- specific: is this one particular thing — a person, a project, a trip, a
  purchase, an event — that a search could actually find more of? "Work",
  "Family", "Around the house", "Money" are NOT: they are parts of life, and
  a search for them returns everything. If not, set false and say in `why`,
  in ten words, what would make it specific. Give no queries.
- queries: two to four searches. They are matched as WORDS, not as questions,
  so each is one to three distinctive words — a name, a product, a place.
  Different angles, not rewordings.
"""

SWEEP_JUDGE = """A person keeps a subject and a search has turned up candidates. Decide
which of them genuinely belong to it.

Answer ONLY with JSON: {"keep": [{"key": "...", "why": "..."}]}

Be strict. A candidate belongs only if the person, reading the subject, would
say "yes, that is part of this" — the same project, the same trip, the same
person about the same matter. Sharing a word is not belonging. Most
candidates do not belong, and an empty list is a good answer. At most five.
`why` is ten words at most on what ties it to the subject.
"""


def sweep(user, sid, act):
    """Run one subject's sweep and file what it offers. OFF the request path."""
    def note(text, found=None):
        def fn(subs):
            for x in subs:
                if x["sid"] == sid:
                    x["sweep"] = {"note": text, "at": datetime.now().isoformat(timespec="minutes")}
                    if found is not None:
                        x["found"] = found
        edit_subjects(user, fn)

    try:
        me = next((x for x in load_subjects(user)["subjects"] if x["sid"] == sid), None)
        if not me:
            return
        try:
            known = json.load(open(ask_path(user, "things")))
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
            known = {}
        inside = [known[i] for i in me["ids"] if i in known] + [r["what"] for r in me.get("pinned") or []]
        brief_ = f"SUBJECT: {me['title']}\nIN IT:\n" + "\n".join(f"- {t}" for t in inside[:20])

        def model(system, content):
            text = ask_claude(system, [{"role": "user", "content": content}],
                              work="periodic_check", max_tokens=500).strip()
            return json.loads(text[text.index("{"):text.rindex("}") + 1])

        plan = model(SWEEP_PLAN, brief_)
        queries = [str(q).strip()[:60] for q in (plan.get("queries") or []) if str(q).strip()][:4]
        if not plan.get("specific") or not queries:
            why = str(plan.get("why") or "").strip().rstrip(".")
            return note("Too broad to sweep" + (f" — {why}." if why else ".")
                        + " Rename it to one person, project or trip.")

        seen = set(me.get("dismissed") or []) | {r["key"] for r in me.get("pinned") or []}
        seen |= {f"mail:{i[1:]}" for i in me["ids"] if i.startswith("m")}
        seen |= {f"feed:{i[1:]}" for i in me["ids"] if i.startswith("n")}
        cands = {}
        for q in queries:
            try:
                for r in (json.loads(tool("search_everything", {"query": q, "limit": 8}, act))
                          .get("results") or []):
                    key = f"{r.get('source')}:{r.get('id')}"
                    if key not in seen and r.get("title"):
                        cands.setdefault(key, {
                            "key": key, "what": str(r["title"])[:110],
                            "source": str(r.get("source_label") or r.get("source") or ""),
                            "icon": str(r.get("icon") or "doc"), "route": str(r.get("route") or ""),
                            "snippet": str(r.get("snippet") or "")[:160],
                            "date": str(r.get("updated_at") or "")[:10]})
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — one search failing is not the sweep failing
                print(f"today: sweep search failed: {type(e).__name__}", flush=True)
            try:
                hits = json.loads(tool("email", {"action": "search", "query": q, "limit": 8}, act))
                for m_ in hits if isinstance(hits, list) else (hits.get("messages") or hits.get("results") or []):
                    key = f"mail:{m_.get('id') or m_.get('gmail_id')}"
                    who, _addr = sender(m_.get("from"))
                    if key not in seen and m_.get("subject"):
                        cands.setdefault(key, {
                            "key": key, "what": f"{who} — {m_['subject']}"[:110], "source": "Mail",
                            "icon": "envelope", "route": f"/inbox?selectedId={key[5:]}",
                            "snippet": str(m_.get("snippet") or "")[:160],
                            "date": str(m_.get("date") or m_.get("received_at") or "")[:10]})
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
                print(f"today: sweep mail search failed: {type(e).__name__}", flush=True)
        if not cands:
            return note(f"Swept {plural(len(queries), 'search', 'searches')} — nothing new.", [])

        listing = "\n".join(f"{c['key']} | {c['source']} | {c['what']} | {c['snippet']} | {c['date']}"
                            for c in list(cands.values())[:30])
        verdict = model(SWEEP_JUDGE, brief_ + "\n\nCANDIDATES — `key | source | title | snippet | date`:\n" + listing)
        found = []
        for k in (verdict.get("keep") or [])[:5]:
            c = cands.get(str(k.get("key"))) if isinstance(k, dict) else None
            if c:
                found.append({"key": c["key"], "what": c["what"], "source": c["source"],
                              "icon": c["icon"], "route": c["route"],
                              "why": str(k.get("why") or "").strip()[:90]})
        note(f"Found {plural(len(found), 'thing')} that may belong." if found
             else f"Swept {plural(len(queries), 'search', 'searches')}, {plural(len(cands), 'result')} — "
                  "nothing that belongs.", found)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        print(f"today: sweep failed: {type(e).__name__}: {e}", flush=True)
        note(f"The sweep couldn't finish — {plainly(e) if not isinstance(e, Gap) else e}.")
    finally:
        with _ask_lock:
            _sweeping.discard((user, sid))
        nudge()


def write_brief(user, key, context, new_ids=(), rekey=None):
    """Ask where the new things go, and for the words. OFF the request path."""
    try:
        system = ORGANISE_SYSTEM
        told = fixes(user)
        if told:
            system += ("\nTHIS PERSON'S STANDING RULES. They OVERRIDE your own "
                       "judgment:\n" + "\n".join(f"- {f['text']}" for f in told) + "\n")
        # In batches. Handed 45 new items at once, the model answered for
        # six. Fifteen at a time it answers for all of them, and each batch
        # is shown the subjects the last one opened, so two notices about one
        # release land together instead of opening two subjects.
        head, _sep, tail = context.partition("\n\nNEW ITEMS")
        new_block, sep2, rest = tail.partition("\n\nTODAY'S CALENDAR")
        new_lines = [ln for ln in new_block.splitlines()[1:] if ln.strip() and ln.strip() != "(none)"]
        rest = ("\n\nTODAY'S CALENDAR" + rest) if sep2 else ""
        shown, data, names = False, {}, {}
        batches = [new_lines[i:i + 15] for i in range(0, len(new_lines), 15)] or [[]]
        lines = lambda v: [str(x).strip()[:120] for x in (v or []) if str(x).strip()][:3]  # noqa: E731
        for n, batch in enumerate(batches):
            opened = "\n".join(f"{x['sid']} | {x['title']}" for x in load_subjects(user)["subjects"])
            body = (head + ("\n\nSUBJECTS OPENED SO FAR (use `into` for these):\n" + opened if n and opened else "")
                    + "\n\nNEW ITEMS — place every one (`id | source | what | when | detail`):\n"
                    + ("\n".join(batch) or "(none)") + rest)
            text = ask_claude(system, [{"role": "user", "content": body}],
                              work="periodic_check", max_tokens=1800).strip()
            data = json.loads(text[text.index("{"):text.rindex("}") + 1])
            ids_here = {ln.split(" | ", 1)[0].strip() for ln in batch} & set(new_ids)
            with _regen_lock:
                got_names, got_shown = apply_places(
                    user, [pl for pl in (data.get("place") or []) if isinstance(pl, dict)], ids_here)
            names.update(got_names)
            shown = shown or got_shown
        with _regen_lock:
            try:
                old = json.load(open(brief_path(user)))
            except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
                old = None
            states = dict((old or {}).get("states") or {})
            states.update({names.get(str(k), str(k)): str(v).strip()[:140]
                           for k, v in (data.get("states") or {}).items() if str(v).strip()})
            visible = {i for x in load_subjects(user)["subjects"] for i in x["ids"]}
            filed = {"digest": rekey(visible) if rekey else key, "states": states,
                     "focus": lines(data.get("focus")), "next": lines(data.get("next")),
                     "at": datetime.now().isoformat(timespec="minutes")}
            # Twenty promotions a day arrive, each one "new". If filing one as
            # noise also rewrote the summary, the words at the top of the page
            # would change twenty times a day for nothing. When nothing new
            # reached the page and the day itself has not moved, the summary
            # is left exactly as it was.
            if old and new_ids and not shown and old.get("digest") == filed["digest"]:
                filed.update(focus=old.get("focus") or [], next=old.get("next") or [],
                             states=old.get("states") or {}, at=old.get("at"))
            json.dump(filed, open(brief_path(user), "w"))
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — costs the words, not the page
        print(f"today: organising failed: {type(e).__name__}: {e}", flush=True)
    finally:
        with _regen_lock:
            _regen.discard((user, key))
        nudge()   # the open page was drawn before this existed


def write_answer(user, question, context):
    """Answer one question and file it. Runs OFF the request path.

    Same shape as `write_brief`, and for the same reason: a model call is
    seconds and a descriptor action is something a finger is waiting on. The
    press files the question, this writes the answer, and `nudge()` tells the
    open page to refetch — which is the machinery the briefing already uses,
    so there is one way this page gets late news instead of two.
    """
    rec = {"question": question, "at": datetime.now().isoformat(timespec="minutes")}
    try:
        rec["text"] = ask_claude(
            ASK_SYSTEM,
            [{"role": "user", "content": f"{context}\n\nTHEIR QUESTION: {question}"}],
            work="periodic_check", max_tokens=500).strip()
        if not rec["text"]:
            raise ValueError("the model answered with nothing")
    except Gap as e:
        rec["failed"] = str(e)
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 — a question that fails costs a line
        rec["failed"] = f"{type(e).__name__}"
        print(f"today: answer failed: {type(e).__name__}: {e}", flush=True)
    try:
        json.dump(rec, open(ask_path(user), "w"))
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001
        print(f"today: could not file the answer: {type(e).__name__}", flush=True)
    with _ask_lock:
        _asking.discard(user)
    nudge()


def answer_for(user, now):
    """What is on the page: a question in flight, an answer, or neither."""
    with _ask_lock:
        in_flight = user in _asking
    try:
        rec = json.load(open(ask_path(user)))
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — nothing asked yet
        rec = None
    if in_flight:
        return None, (rec or {}).get("question", "")
    if not rec:
        return None, ""
    written = when(rec.get("at"))
    mins = int((now - written).total_seconds() // 60) if written else 0
    return {"question": rec.get("question", ""),
            "text": rec.get("text", ""),
            "failed": rec.get("failed", ""),
            "age": ("just now" if mins < 2 else f"{mins} min ago" if mins < 90
                    else f"{mins // 60}h ago" if mins < 24 * 60 else "earlier")}, ""


def ui_action(name, args, user, grant=None):
    """The hub's action channel — `POST /leo/ui/action`.

    Three verbs, and every one of them returns at once: `ask` files the
    question and hands the model call to a thread, `clear` forgets the last
    answer, `rewrite` drops the cached briefing so the next draw rebuilds it.
    Nothing here blocks on a model.
    """
    if name == "ask":
        question = str(args.get("text") or "").strip()[:300]
        if not question:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "no question"}
        try:
            context = open(ask_path(user, "ctx")).read()
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 — never drawn for this person yet
            context = ""
        if not context:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "nothing to answer from yet"}
        with _ask_lock:
            if user in _asking:
                return {"ok": True, "busy": True}
            _asking.add(user)
        json.dump({"question": question, "at": datetime.now().isoformat(timespec="minutes")},
                  open(ask_path(user), "w"))
        threading.Thread(target=write_answer, daemon=True,
                         args=(user, question, context)).start()
        return {"ok": True}
    if name == "clear":
        try:
            os.remove(ask_path(user))
        except OSError:
            pass
        return {"ok": True}
    if name == "rename":
        sid, title = str(args.get("id") or ""), str(args.get("title") or "").strip()[:40]
        if not sid or not title:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "a subject needs a name"}

        def fn(subs):
            for x in subs:
                if x["sid"] == sid:
                    x["title"] = title
                    return True
            return False
        return {"ok": bool(edit_subjects(user, fn))}
    if name == "sweep":
        sid = str(args.get("id") or "")
        if not any(x["sid"] == sid for x in load_subjects(user)["subjects"]):
            return {"ok": False, "error": "no such subject"}
        act = acting(grant)
        if not act:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "no acting credential"}
        with _ask_lock:
            if (user, sid) in _sweeping:
                return {"ok": True, "busy": True}
            _sweeping.add((user, sid))
        threading.Thread(target=sweep, daemon=True, args=(user, sid, act)).start()
        return {"ok": True}
    if name in ("keep", "drop"):
        sid, key = str(args.get("id") or ""), str(args.get("key") or "")

        def fn(subs):
            for x in subs:
                if x["sid"] == sid:
                    hit = next((f for f in x.get("found") or [] if f["key"] == key), None)
                    if not hit:
                        return False
                    x["found"] = [f for f in x["found"] if f["key"] != key]
                    if name == "keep":
                        x.setdefault("pinned", []).append({k: hit[k] for k in ("key", "what", "source", "icon", "route")})
                    else:
                        x.setdefault("dismissed", []).append(key)
                        x["dismissed"] = x["dismissed"][-200:]
                    return True
            return False
        return {"ok": bool(edit_subjects(user, fn))}
    if name in ("done", "later", "mute"):
        iid, what = str(args.get("id") or ""), str(args.get("what") or "")[:120]
        if not iid:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "no item"}
        now = datetime.now(timezone.utc).astimezone()
        st = load_state(user, now)
        bucket = {"done": "done", "later": "later", "mute": "muted"}[name]
        st[bucket][iid] = later_until(now).isoformat() if name == "later" else now.isoformat()
        st["last"] = {"verb": name, "id": iid, "what": what, "at": now.isoformat()}
        if name == "mute" and what:
            add_fix(user, f"Not for me: {what}")
        save_state(user, st)
        return {"ok": True}
    if name == "undo":
        st = load_state(user)
        last = st.get("last")
        if not last:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "nothing to undo"}
        for bucket in ("done", "later", "muted"):
            st[bucket].pop(last["id"], None)
        if last["verb"] == "mute":
            rule = f"Not for me: {last['what']}"
            for f in fixes(user):
                if f["text"] == rule:
                    drop_fix(user, f["id"])
        st["last"] = None
        save_state(user, st)
        return {"ok": True}
    if name == "correct":
        text = str(args.get("text") or "").strip()[:200]
        if not text:
            return {"ok": False, "error": "nothing to correct"}
        add_fix(user, text)
        # Drop the cached briefing: a correction that does not visibly change
        # anything until the digest happens to move is a correction the
        # person has no reason to believe was heard.
        try:
            os.remove(brief_path(user))
        except OSError:
            pass
        return {"ok": True}
    if name == "uncorrect":
        drop_fix(user, str(args.get("id") or ""))
        try:
            os.remove(brief_path(user))
        except OSError:
            pass
        return {"ok": True}
    if name == "rewrite":
        try:
            os.remove(brief_path(user))
        except OSError:
            pass
        return {"ok": True}
    return {"ok": False, "error": f"no such action: {name}"}


def organised(user, key, context, now, new_ids=(), rekey=None):
    """The filed summary, and a pass behind the page when there is something
    new to file or the day has moved. Served stale meanwhile — never blank."""
    try:
        held = json.load(open(brief_path(user)))
        if not isinstance(held.get("states"), dict):
            held = None
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001
        held = None
    stale = bool(held) and held.get("digest") != key
    if new_ids or not held or stale:
        job = (user, key + ":" + str(len(new_ids)))
        with _regen_lock:
            if not any(u == user for u, _k in _regen):
                _regen.add((user, key))
                threading.Thread(target=write_brief, daemon=True,
                                 args=(user, key, context, tuple(new_ids), rekey)).start()
    if held:
        written = when(held.get("at"))
        mins = int((now - written).total_seconds() // 60) if written else 0
        held["age"] = ("just now" if mins < 2 else f"{mins} min ago" if mins < 90
                       else f"{mins // 60}h ago" if mins < 24 * 60 else "yesterday")
        held["stale"] = "1" if stale else ""
    return held


